{"W": {"correctness": 9, "codeQuality": 9, "tests": 8, "robustnessSecurity": 9, "docsUsability": 9, "overall": 9, "notes": "Fixes: lib/validate.mjs:64 Math.round (4.35 -> 435 cents) and lib/ledger.mjs:10-13 leftover cents to the first people in split; probe showed 4.35 and shares 3.34/3.33/3.33 with balances summing to 0. lib/import.mjs:33-46 checks the body (unknown field via Object.keys order, entries 1-50, then dry_run, then skip_recorded, with Object.hasOwn so null is checked); checkEntry at :50-68 turns the existing parsers' ValidationErrors into rule names via parseAs at :23; brokenRule at :75-81 adds the key to `seen` before the already-recorded check, so earlier duplicate or already-recorded entries still count; the reply at :139 saves nothing for a dry run or an all-skipped import. My ~35-case HTTP probe (rule order, duplicate and already-recorded with and without skip_recorded, dry-run ids, all-skipped not writing the file, 413, __proto__, a 422 not writing) gave correct replies, identical to the other three. Tests: npm test 76/76; test/import.test.mjs has 40 tests, including body-size bytes vs characters (:46-66), PATCH/DELETE effects on already-recorded (:308-320), restart persistence (:427) and the id after a dry run (:491). README adds the table row, a full Importing section and the qualified unknown-fields sentence, and lists lib/import.mjs in the layout.", "X": {"correctness": 9, "codeQuality": 8, "tests": 7, "robustnessSecurity": 9, "docsUsability": 9, "overall": 8, "notes": "Same fixes (lib/validate.mjs:64, lib/ledger.mjs:10-13, using cents % people.length). lib/import.mjs:33-50 entryProblem returns the rule name; the typeof guard at :36 is a careful touch. planImport at :71-90 then calls parseExpense again on an entry it already validated (and parseAmount again for payments at :78): redundant double parsing, though correct. Ids and the saving decision at :100-120 are correct. My probe gave correct replies identical to the others. Tests: npm test 60/60; test/import.test.mjs has 17 broader tests covering body order, 413, rules, duplicate after a bad entry, already-recorded after PATCH/DELETE (:221), skip, all-skipped not writing (:248), dry run and restart (:285). Fewer boundary cases than W or Z. README is complete and also states that a duplicate or skipped earlier entry still counts.", "Y": {"correctness": 9, "codeQuality": 8, "tests": 6, "robustnessSecurity": 9, "docsUsability": 8, "overall": 7, "notes": "Same fixes (lib/validate.mjs:64, lib/ledger.mjs:10-13). lib/import.mjs:75-135 puts the whole classification in one forEach inside the handler: readable but less modular than the others. The Object.hasOwn(entry,'split') at :46 is redundant, since JSON cannot hold undefined. Ids at :112-116 and the saving decision at :134 are correct. My probe gave correct replies identical to the others. Tests: npm test 49/49, but test/import.test.mjs has only 8 tests (182 lines). They do cover body order including null flags (:43-60), 413 (:60), a large table of rules (:64), already-recorded and duplicate with skip (:115), all-skipped not writing (:135), dry run (:157) and id gaps (:170). They are thin on edge cases (no PATCH/DELETE effects, no other-group check, no restart). README is complete, though it does not say that a duplicate or skipped earlier entry still counts.", "Z": {"correctness": 9, "codeQuality": 8, "tests": 10, "robustnessSecurity": 9, "docsUsability": 9, "overall": 9, "notes": "Same fixes (lib/validate.mjs:64, lib/ledger.mjs:10-13). lib/import.mjs is cleanly split into parseBody (:50), parseEntry (:62) and review (:83), with ids handed out at :125-129; the NOT_SHOWN constant at :16 explains the unused messages. Small style nit: `isObject =v` at :18. My probe gave correct replies identical to the others. Tests: npm test 137/137 across fixes.test.mjs (the coffee and taxi reports end to end, including the cents on disk), import-checks (413 and 400 order, __proto__, Object.keys order), import-rules (each rule and its order), import-dedupe (31 cases: case and whitespace, 1-cent differences, other group, deleted, PATCH, the task's two-entries example with skip true, false and absent, all-skipped not writing even with a dry run) and import.test (key order, restart, no temp file left, the id after a dry run). The most thorough suite here. README is complete; its 404 step omits the error body text, which is minor."}, "ranking": ["Z", "W", "X", "Y"]}
```

